9% to win", "Trump's attacks on Cuellar and corruption-focused GOP messaging keep the race competitive", "Thin and dated polling — only one usable poll from August"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a modest Cuellar edge: incumbency and money offset a Republican-leaning district, and markets plus most raters put this in Likely/Lean D territory. Trump's attacks and Tijerina's self-funding-style campaign add risk, but not enough to move the race to a tossup.", "watch": "Any late independent polling or a surge of outside spending against Cuellar over the corruption line could tighten this toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 49 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Henry Cuellar (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Ten-term incumbent Henry Cuellar defends a South Texas border seat that leans slightly Republican, against Republican Tano Tijerina and a Green Party candidate. Cuellar holds a clear money advantage and the benefits of incumbency, and prediction markets price his win chances near 89 percent. Most expert raters place the race between Tilt D and Likely D.\n\nThe race carries unusual friction: President Trump has publicly attacked Cuellar despite having pardoned him, and Tijerina campaigns hard on corruption themes, while a key Latino advocacy group offered only a tepid endorsement. Polling is sparse and somewhat dated, with the one usable survey from August showing Cuellar up eleven. The forecast expects Cuellar to win by a moderate margin, with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9009, "margin": 7.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8765, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8779, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8804, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8627, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8617, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8973, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8